00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:42.620
Imagine you bought a TSX energy name last week after it bounced from a recent low. Your order filled near $52, but the stock reversed the next session.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:51.580
What actually happened was that price tested the 50-day moving average twice without enough volume to break through, turning that level into resistance.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:03:00.740
Professionals always check whether the bounce occurred on volume at least 50% above the 20-day average before treating the move as confirmed.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:07.540
Most retail investors skip this step and end up buying into failed breakouts that look like support on a quick glance.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:17.980
The concrete fix is to mark the prior swing high and low on the daily chart and only add exposure once price closes above resistance on elevated volume.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:26.660
Support forms where buyers have repeatedly stepped in at a particular price, creating a floor that can be tested multiple times before it breaks.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:33.840
Resistance appears at prior highs where sellers have defended the level, often requiring stronger volume to overcome.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:43.260
Moving averages such as the 50-day and 200-day serve as dynamic versions of these levels because they shift daily and reflect recent price action.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:50.780
Volume acts as the confirming signal because low volume tests frequently fail while high volume breaks tend to hold.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:04:00.260
The honest limitation is that these levels work until a catalyst changes the underlying supply and demand balance, at which point the chart must be redrawn.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.560
Retail traders gain an edge by waiting for the close above resistance rather than entering on the first touch.

